  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1113 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nA strong upper level low will navigate south/southeast tonight \ninto Thursday, with the low center expected to track across SW MO \nand NW AR. While the surface cyclone is likely to intensify well \neast of our area, guidance continues to indicate wrap around \nmoisture will move into NW AR and perhaps far NE OK Thanksgiving \nmorning and afternoon. Light precipitation may develop by mid \nThursday morning, with potential continuing into late afternoon. \nThermal profiles will support snow as the dominant precip type, \nespecially early. Temperatures will warm during the day, but will \nlikely struggle in areas with prolonged cloud cover and \nprecipitation. Thus, if precipitation extends this far west, \npredominantly light snow is expected with some rain mixing in \nduring the afternoon. Winds will respond and become gusty out of \nthe northwest late tonight and Thursday as well.\n\nModel guidance has been inconsistent with how far west \nprecipitation extends with this system, and this will make or \nbreak the snowfall and temperature forecast tomorrow. For now, QPF\ncontinues to look quite light for our area, with higher amounts \nto the north and east. Still, given sfc temps in the low-mid 30s, \nup to a half inch of snow is possible across the higher terrain of\nCarroll and northern Madison counties. Lighter amounts cannot be \nruled out across eastern Benton and Washington counties as well. \nFor now, the expectation is that any travel impacts should remain \nminor and isolated given light precipitation rates and marginal \nsurface temperatures.\n\nTransitory sfc ridging develops Thursday night into Friday \nmorning before southwest flow returns to the region. This will \nwarm things up nicely on Friday and Saturday, with high temps \nwarming into the upper 50s and lower 60s (upper 60s across SE OK).\nBy late Saturday, another trough and associated sfc low will \nswing into the plains and drag a cold front through E OK and W AR.\nMoisture return ahead of this system appears brief, but at least \nlow PoPs are warranted across far E OK and NW AR Saturday night \ninto Sunday. There may be a couple rumbles of thunder, but the \nbetter instability likely remains east of the FA.\n\nFollowing this, another wave may move through the region early \nnext week, but with little or no impact due to low moisture. \nOtherwise, heights will tend to increase with dry conditions \nfavored. Temperatures will rebound back to near or above average \nby mid to late week.",
